WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.590
- All right, so you brought your son

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:03.240
to set with you while shooting, right?

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.073
- I always do.

00:00:04.073 --> 00:00:08.160
My son's almost five and both
of us, me and my husband,

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.890
are both directors, so he's
kind of grown up going to sets,

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.730
and he gave little tips
sometimes to Tom, like--

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:15.530
- Oh my, Tom Hanks (laughs)?
- Yeah

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:17.933
Sometimes he was like,

00:00:17.933 --> 00:00:19.197
"You know, I like what you're doing,

00:00:19.197 --> 00:00:21.327
"I think you need a rocket ship".

00:00:21.327 --> 00:00:22.980
(all laughing)

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.387
- Tom was like "Okay, great,
great, what else, what else?

00:00:25.387 --> 00:00:26.650
"Tell me what else".

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:27.530
- [Kelly] Oh my God!

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:28.363
- Yeah.

00:00:28.363 --> 00:00:30.090
- Two directors, that's amazing.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:30.923
- Yeah.

00:00:30.923 --> 00:00:32.820
- That has got to be a
fun relationship ma'am.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.751
- It is, it means we
understand each others work.

00:00:35.751 --> 00:00:36.584
- [Kelly] That is cool.

00:00:36.584 --> 00:00:38.170
- We know what the other
one's going through.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:39.003
- Really cool.

00:00:39.003 --> 00:00:40.754
- Story time must be great
for Wiley, like at night

00:00:40.754 --> 00:00:41.593
- Yeah, I know.

00:00:41.593 --> 00:00:43.947
- Story like "What did
mum say last night?"

00:00:43.947 --> 00:00:46.110
"Right, well, in my version".

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.517
- All the time.

00:00:47.517 --> 00:00:48.350
Everything's a competition.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:49.433
- Yeah, yeah.

00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:51.500
- That is our household as well (laughs).

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.670
So how did this movie make you

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:55.840
reflect on your own life?

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:56.673
- Well I mean like you,

00:00:56.673 --> 00:00:58.090
I grew up watching Mr. Rogers,

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:00.120
but I think the thing about Mr. Rogers,

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.310
is it's also, we watched
it when we were so little.

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.210
It's almost like in our DNA.

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:06.010
It's like pre-memory.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.330
So I feel like I got to
rediscover Mr. Rogers

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:10.630
as a grownup and as a parent, you know?

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:11.497
- As a parent?

00:01:11.497 --> 00:01:12.330
- It's a different thing

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.190
to look at the show now

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.000
and to see it through the eyes

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.100
of a grown person, and to recognize,

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.830
oh wow, he's talking about things

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.630
a lot of us still need to work on.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.250
- I mean literally, my husband and I

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:26.188
sat and sobbed in our
bedroom watching this film.

00:01:26.188 --> 00:01:27.021
- I'm glad.

00:01:27.021 --> 00:01:28.560
- Because he is very close to my heart

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:29.490
because I didn't grow up with a father,

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.250
so for a lot of us it's a father figure,

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:32.083
you know?

00:01:32.083 --> 00:01:33.130
- I've had so many people say that to me,

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:34.700
that they almost actually believed

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:35.970
Mr. Rogers was their dad

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:36.803
when they were growing up.
- Yes.

00:01:36.803 --> 00:01:37.880
Or it was somebody that you wanted,

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.396
you thought that's what it should be like.

00:01:39.396 --> 00:01:40.300
You know?

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.670
You did a really great job.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:42.640
- Like Bill and Joanne,

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:43.870
Joanne Rogers, his wife

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:45.950
and Bill who was his right
hand man for his whole life,

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:47.050
said something to me the other day

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:47.883
in Pittsburgh, where they said,

00:01:47.883 --> 00:01:49.587
"You know, Fred didn't expect

00:01:49.587 --> 00:01:50.857
"everybody to live in his image.

00:01:50.857 --> 00:01:52.637
"He accepted us with
all our imperfections.

00:01:52.637 --> 00:01:53.997
"He knew we weren't perfect parents.

00:01:53.997 --> 00:01:55.880
"He knew we weren't perfect people."

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:58.450
Like, something is
freeing about recognizing

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:00.084
both that Fred was a human being,

00:02:00.084 --> 00:02:01.428
he wasn't a saint,

00:02:01.428 --> 00:02:04.780
and that he didn't expect
us to be saints either.

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:07.960
He didn't shy away from the hard parts

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:09.670
of being alive and being a human being.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:11.670
- And that was actually
one of my favorite parts

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:12.940
in the film is where his wife

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.997
does say, "He has to work at this.

00:02:14.997 --> 00:02:15.970
"This isn't like,--

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:17.190
- She said that to me.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:18.067
- I know.

00:02:18.067 --> 00:02:18.900
- That's in the movie
because she said that to me.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:19.950
- And that makes hope for all of us,

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.030
because I'm like, if you just come out

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.270
being awesome and lovely, well great,

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:24.365
that's easy for you.

00:02:24.365 --> 00:02:26.680
But knowing he had to work at it,

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:27.870
we all have to work at it.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:28.780
It takes effort.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:29.830
- It's like to take time

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:32.254
to listen to other people
and empathize with them,

00:02:32.254 --> 00:02:33.840
takes more work.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.800
- One funny thing I found
about this though, is that

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.920
I heard Tom Hanks turned
this role down a few times--

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:40.063
- He did.

00:02:40.063 --> 00:02:40.896
- but then somehow you roped him in.

00:02:40.896 --> 00:02:41.940
How did you rope him in and

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:42.825
can I know your secret?

00:02:42.825 --> 00:02:43.739
(audience laughing)

00:02:43.739 --> 00:02:45.810
- I'll never do anything so miraculous

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:46.643
again in my life.

00:02:46.643 --> 00:02:50.380
The story is that, I'm friends
with Tom Hanks' son Colin.

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:51.213
- Oh, okay.

00:02:51.213 --> 00:02:52.210
- And Tom and I met

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:53.340
at a backyard birthday party

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.090
where all the kids where
playing in the backyard.

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:56.027
And we were talking and he said,

00:02:56.027 --> 00:02:56.860
"Oh, what do you do?"

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:57.804
and I said,

00:02:57.804 --> 00:02:58.637
"Well, I'm a director".

00:02:58.637 --> 00:02:59.987
And he said "Oh, there
was just this article

00:02:59.987 --> 00:03:01.337
"in the New York Times this weekend

00:03:01.337 --> 00:03:02.938
"about women directors in Hollywood."

00:03:02.938 --> 00:03:05.647
And I said, "Yeah, I'm in that article".

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:08.009
- How cool did you feel?

00:03:08.009 --> 00:03:08.980
With Tom Hanks standing there

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:09.947
how cool did you feel, like

00:03:09.947 --> 00:03:12.427
"Actually, I'm in the article.

00:03:12.427 --> 00:03:13.600
"No big deal, Tom."

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.260
- No big deal, Tom Hanks.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:16.630
And he said, "I'll watch your movie"

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:18.870
and a week later he personally emailed me

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:19.703
and said, "I watched your movie.

00:03:19.703 --> 00:03:20.910
"Let's get together and chat."

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:21.890
And that the other thing about him,

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:23.070
is that he doesn't have like someone

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:25.290
reach out to you, like he
reaches out to you and says.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:26.123
- That's so cool.

00:03:26.123 --> 00:03:27.290
So he must have loved your work.

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:29.140
- And then we kept in touch for years

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.420
and when I signed on to this movie,

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:31.253
the Producer said,

00:03:31.253 --> 00:03:33.077
"Tom Hanks has always been
our dream as Mr. Rogers

00:03:33.077 --> 00:03:34.897
"but he's passed on the movie three times.

00:03:34.897 --> 00:03:35.730
"He said no."

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:38.240
And I said, "Well I could try".

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.180
And I reached out to him
and he (finger clicks)

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:41.440
signed on right away.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:42.932
- [Kelly] You're magical.

00:03:42.932 --> 00:03:43.765
You're magical!

00:03:43.765 --> 00:03:44.788
(audience clapping)

00:03:44.788 --> 00:03:45.621
- [Interviewee] And I'm just so grateful!

00:03:45.621 --> 00:03:47.360
- [Kelly] He is perfect.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:48.480
- [Interviewee] He is.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:49.870
- The casting was perfect.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:51.820
The directing was amazing.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:53.050
- Everybody who worked on this movie

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:54.643
worked on it because they loved it.

00:03:54.643 --> 00:03:55.890
And you could feel it.
- It reads through the camera.

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:57.830
Yeah, you can feel it and it matters.

00:03:57.830 --> 00:03:58.680
- It does matter.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.530
You know I really believe
you don't have to suffer

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:01.440
to make good art.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:02.273
- Yes.

00:04:02.273 --> 00:04:03.123
Oh I love you.

